# Paths exempt from leakscan (gitignore-style globs, one per line).
# Keep this list SHORT and reasoned — every entry is a hole in the boundary.

# The scanner's own unit tests are, by definition, a wall of leak-SHAPED
# fixtures (emails, MACs, addresses) whose whole job is to prove detection.
# They are kept deliberately fictional; scanning them only yields noise.
tools/test_leakscan.py
# signscan's tests carry example committer addresses (an example.test author and
# GitHub's web-flow no-reply) in throwaway repos — fixtures, not real addresses.
tools/test_signscan.py

# secretscan's tests are the same: a wall of fictional credential shapes, which
# are also leak-shaped (emails/keys). Exempt them for the same reason.
tools/test_secretscan.py

# The local-term list TEMPLATE is, like the tests, a wall of deliberately
# fictional example shapes documenting the format. No real data lives here.
tools/leakscan-terms.example.txt

# The canonical signing trust list carries a principal email beside each public
# key by the allowed_signers format's own design (SIGNING.md, ADR 0007) — the
# emails ARE the data the file exists to hold, and every future key appended
# would re-trigger the same finding. Public keys + principals only; secretscan
# still covers it against a real private key ever being pasted in.
allowed_signers

# The plugin + marketplace manifests carry the author's own name in the
# author/owner attribution field — legitimate, approved, named-worked-example
# identity (ADR 0005; an adopter substitutes their own).
# This is the same case the CLAUDE.md convention line handles with an inline
# `leakscan:allow`, but JSON cannot hold that marker, so the exemption lives
# here. Scope is deliberately tight (two small structured manifests) and
# secretscan still covers them for credentials.
.claude-plugin/plugin.json
.claude-plugin/marketplace.json
